  "notes": "Every arm runs on one shared warm client - the same pool key - so the transport is as close to identical as the stack allows and only generation-side settings vary. Arm A is the verified control request byte for byte. Arms B to E each change exactly one thing from it: thinking disabled, effort low, a first-sentence directive, or a lower token cap. The tuned generator at neutral settings produces the control's request exactly, which a test pins - that is what makes 'one change' true. Held constant: claude-sonnet-5, the six frozen packets, the benchmark system prompt except where arm D appends to it, the 25-word chunk rule, no TTS. Arm E is the one that can fail on its own terms: a lower cap could truncate before 25 words, so stop_reason is recorded and the report flags any truncated trial rather than reporting a first chunk that never completed as a fast one."
